<p>An American Airlines flight operated by Envoy Air landed Thursday at Simón Bolívar International Airport near Caracas, restoring direct commercial passenger service between the United States and Venezuela after a seven-year suspension. Flight AA3599 departed Miami at about 10:26 a.m. and arrived near Caracas around 1:15 p.m. local time, carrying U.S. officials, business executives, journalists and other passengers. The route reopened after U.S. officials in January lifted the 2019 order that had barred U.S. carriers from flying to Venezuela over security concerns, following President Trump&#x27;s direction. The resumption follows the U.S. capture of then-President Nicolás Maduro in Caracas in early January and the restoration of diplomatic relations in March, with the U.S. embassy in Caracas formally reopened about a month before the flight. U.S. officials on the inaugural flight included Jarrod Agen, director of the U.S. National Energy Dominance Council, who was scheduled to meet Venezuelan officials and energy and mining executives as Washington seeks to expand U.S. business activity in Venezuela. American Airlines said it would operate daily service on the route and add a second daily Miami-Caracas flight on May 21.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/bhBSoTbL4xYFDpEGttuo0O9yBUDxidc_KSMzNmrKMCqMifVOwuEhLDOiddcw-D1L0OprvaI_5JX_qBUaOAIVEVhnAolZYjpR-WvjjoMi158OQ2TNlT-xptgLe9Q-tg7QIdjXqpwvyjxFrjBCXgadU7gpfptTrmxQovM8kyUVjhiKJmf3NCl7dQxor488kT1-SHpplkpHgriWJIZNrRdIgqEsWO7rqn-6zzCJIpAJAg8rKAIfHzUwclQ-SbrZ28ZWLJBt4UzWkuvkjIGSQyfvzR4MWr2Cpsg' alt='The captain waves a Venezuelan flag and the first officer holds a U.S. flag before the first direct commercial flight between the United States and Venezuela in seven years.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Regulatory process: American announced plans to restore the route in January, and the Transportation Department later approved its request for daily Miami-Caracas service in March.</li><li>Passenger links: About 1.2 million Venezuelans live in the United States, and Miami remains a major hub for Latin American travel.</li><li>Launch ceremony: The aircraft received water-cannon salutes at departure and landing, and the pilot displayed a Venezuelan flag from the cockpit window as ground crews applauded.</li><li>Airline history: American Airlines began Venezuela service in 1987 and had been the largest U.S. airline operating in the country before the 2019 suspension.</li><li>Migration policy: President Trump has also moved to end a program shielding some Venezuelans in the United States from deportation, according to Dawn.</li></ul><blockquote>Estados Unidos y Venezuela están recuperando una arteria comercial fundamental que va a acelerar la inversión - John Barrett</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>U.S. diplomatic mission: John Barrett framed the restart as another historic milestone in bilateral relations as officials presented the route as part of normalization. (<a href='https://www.nacion.com/el-mundo/aerolineas-reanudan-vuelos-comerciales-entre/5KLCINHCXBFJHGIXDWXZB7FGYQ/story/'>La Nación</a>)</li><li>American Airlines: At the Miami gate, airline staff handed passengers small Venezuelan flags and decorated the boarding area with yellow, blue and red balloons. (<a href='https://www.nbcmiami.com/news/local/flights-resume-from-miami-to-caracas-venezuela-for-the-first-time-in-7-years/3803307/'>NBC Miami</a>)</li><li>Passenger Lennart Ochoa: Ochoa, a Miami passenger, said before boarding that he was excited to see family and the country, adding that he bought a ticket as soon as seats became available. (<a href='https://www.nbcmiami.com/news/local/flights-resume-from-miami-to-caracas-venezuela-for-the-first-time-in-7-years/3803307/'>NBC Miami</a>)</li><li>U.S. State Department: The department framed the restored Miami-Caracas service as an achievement of the Trump administration in a social media post marking the first day of flights. (<a href='https://tocka.com.mk/ves